# ============================================================================
#  SevenFold — configuration
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the two API keys. Everything else is
#  OPTIONAL: every knob below has a default (shown in brackets) that matches the
#  current pipeline, so an .env with just the two keys behaves exactly as-is.
#
#  To change a setting: uncomment the line and edit the value.
#  NOTE: the dev server reads .env at startup — restart it after any change.
# ============================================================================


# ─────────────────────────────  API keys (required)  ────────────────────────

# Anthropic (Claude) — https://console.anthropic.com
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Google (Gemini) — https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=


# ──────────────────  Supabase — accounts & saved analyses  ──────────────────
#
# Optional. Without these the app still runs analyses end to end; it just can't
# sign anyone in or save a report, and the "recent analyses" list stays empty.
#
# Both values are public by design — they ship to the browser. A publishable key
# is not a secret: Row-Level Security is what protects the data, and every policy
# is scoped to `auth.uid()`.
#
# NEVER put a `sb_secret_…` or `service_role` key in either of these two vars.
# Those bypass RLS entirely, and NEXT_PUBLIC_ vars are compiled into the
# JavaScript sent to every visitor. (There is exactly one narrow exception,
# used nowhere near these two — see SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY below.)
#
# Where to find them — https://supabase.com/dashboard
#   URL: Project Settings → Data API
#   Key: Project Settings → API Keys → "Publishable and secret API keys" tab →
#        Publishable key → `sb_publishable_…`
#
# Use the publishable key, not the older `anon` JWT on the "Legacy anon,
# service_role" tab. Both work — supabase-js accepts either — but Supabase is
# retiring the legacy format. Don't hit "Disable JWT-based API keys" until the
# app is confirmed working, so a failure can't be two changes at once.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# The one deliberate exception to "no secret keys". Supabase's SDK has no
# self-service "delete my own account" call — the only way to remove an
# auth.users row is the admin API (`auth.admin.deleteUser`), which requires
# this key. Used in exactly one place: app/api/account/delete/route.ts, which
# reads the caller's own id from their session before ever touching this
# client, so a bug here can only let someone delete their own account, never
# anyone else's. Server-only — deliberately NOT prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, so it
# is never inlined into the browser bundle. Same API Keys page as above,
# "Publishable and secret API keys" tab → secret key → `sb_secret_…`.
# Required whenever the two vars above are set: without it the account page's
# "Delete account" fails with an error naming this variable. Add it to the
# Vercel project's environment variables too, not just the local .env.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=sb_secret_...

# One-time project setup (Authentication section of the dashboard):
#   • Providers → enable Google. (See components/features/auth/providers.ts
#     to add another OAuth provider.)
#   • Providers → Email: leave it enabled with "Confirm email" on, and set the
#     minimum password length to 8 to match the sign-up form.
#   • Sign In / Providers → enable Anonymous sign-ins
#   • Advanced → enable Manual linking (needed to upgrade an anonymous account)
#   • Emails → SMTP Settings → set up a custom SMTP sender (Resend, Postmark…).
#     Supabase's built-in one only delivers to members of your Supabase
#     organization, and only a handful of emails an hour.
#   • Emails → Templates → paste in the files from supabase/templates (its
#     README lists which is which, with subjects).
#   • URL Configuration → Site URL: https://<your-domain>. Redirect URLs:
#     http://localhost:3000/** and https://<your-domain>/** (the callback URL
#     carries a query string, which an exact entry doesn't match).
#   • OAuth Server → enable it, set Authorization Path to /oauth/consent and
#     turn on "Allow Dynamic OAuth Apps". This is what lets Claude connect to
#     the MCP server at /mcp: Claude registers itself with Supabase, which
#     sends the user to <Site URL>/oauth/consent to approve. Nothing to add to
#     .env — the access tokens it issues are verified against the URL above.
# Then apply the files in supabase/migrations in order (0002 limits what an
# approved OAuth app's token can do — apply it before enabling the OAuth Server).


# ─────────────────────────────  Site  ───────────────────────────────────────

# Canonical public origin, used as `metadataBase` for absolute Open Graph /
# Twitter image URLs in link previews (WhatsApp, iMessage, X, LinkedIn, Slack).
# Must include the scheme and no trailing slash.
# [https://vcanalyst.williamragnarsson.dev]
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://vcanalyst.williamragnarsson.dev


# ───────────────────────────────  Models  ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Pick the model for each pipeline stage. Claude vs Gemini is auto-detected from
# the model name (anything starting with "gemini" → Gemini, otherwise Claude),
# so there's nothing else to set.
#
# Valid model ids (copy exactly — an unrecognised id fails the run immediately):
#   claude-opus-5-5        Claude, most capable, most expensive  (~$4 / $20 per 1M tokens in/out)
#   claude-sonnet-5-5      Claude, the step up for judgment      (~$2 / $10)
#   claude-haiku-5-5       Claude, cheapest — the default        (~$0.10 / $0.50; $0.50 / $2.50 past 100K prompt tokens)
#   claude-opus-4-8        Claude, previous Opus                 (~$5 / $25)
#   claude-sonnet-5        Claude, strong + newer                (~$2 / $10)
#   claude-sonnet-4-6      Claude, strong                        (~$3 / $15)
#   claude-haiku-4-5       Claude, previous Haiku                (~$1 / $5)
#   gemini-3.6-flash       Gemini, the step up for judgment      (~$0.75 / $3.75 until 2026-12-31, then $1.50 / $7.50)
#   gemini-3.5-flash       Gemini, strong, pricier than 3.6      (~$1.50 / $9)
#   gemini-3.5-flash-lite  Gemini, cheap + fast                  (~$0.30 / $2.50)
#   gemini-3.1-flash-lite  Gemini, cheaper still; shut down 2027-05-07 (~$0.25 / $1.50)
#   gemini-2.5-flash       Gemini, older, cheap                  (~$0.30 / $2.50)
#   gemini-2.5-flash-lite  Gemini, cheapest of all, weakest      (~$0.10 / $0.40)
#
# Web search (the RESEARCH and LANDSCAPE stages) also bills. Claude's is $10 per
# 1,000 searches with no free allowance; at 3 searches a stage that's 6¢ a run,
# about three quarters of an all-Haiku run. On Gemini 3.x,
# 5,000 search queries a month are free (shared across 3.x models), then ~$14 per
# 1,000 queries. On Gemini 2.5, 1,500 grounded prompts a day are free, then ~$35
# per 1,000 prompts. The usage log counts every search at those rates: it can't
# see how much of the free allowance is left, so it shows the most you'd pay.
#
# This pipeline defaults to Claude Haiku 5.5, with Sonnet 5.5 on scorecard and
# feedback (preset C below). Gemini is still
# fully wired up (lib/llm/gemini.ts): pointing any stage at a gemini-* id above
# routes there with no code change, which is also the recovery from a Claude
# outage.
#
# Presets. Rough cost per run for a typical ~20-slide deck with selectable text,
# estimated from the prompt sizes, not measured: add about 1¢ for a scanned deck
# on Haiku (OCR). Copy a block's lines in, leave the rest unset.
#
#   A. Cheapest             ~$0.06/run  ~1,800 runs per $100
#        everything on Haiku, two searches per search stage:
#          SCORECARD_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5
#          FEEDBACK_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5
#          RESEARCH_MAX_SEARCHES=2
#   B. All Haiku            ~$0.08/run  ~1,300 runs per $100
#        everything on Haiku:
#          SCORECARD_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5
#          FEEDBACK_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5
#   C. Default              ~$0.12/run    ~800 runs per $100   ← best value
#        set nothing: the two stages that judge the deck (scorecard, feedback)
#        run on Sonnet, the rest on Haiku. Their prompts are small, so Sonnet
#        costs little there
#   D. Balanced             ~$0.21/run    ~480 runs per $100
#        the default plus Sonnet reading the search results (research reads
#        ~30K tokens of them, so it's the priciest stage to upgrade):
#          RESEARCH_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
#   E. Quality              ~$0.60/run    ~170 runs per $100
#        Sonnet everywhere except Opus on the judgment, thinking a little:
#          OCR_MODEL / EXTRACT_MODEL / RESEARCH_MODEL / COMPLETE_MODEL /
#          LANDSCAPE_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
#          SCORECARD_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5   SCORECARD_THINKING=low
#          FEEDBACK_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5    FEEDBACK_THINKING=low
#   F. Max                  ~$1.20/run     ~85 runs per $100
#        every *_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5, every *_THINKING=medium
#
# Extract, complete, OCR and the landscape map are transcription and form
# filling, where Haiku does about as well as the bigger models; spend on
# scorecard and feedback first. The dev cost sidebar and the server log
# (💰 usage:) show what a real run cost, stage by stage.
#
# Adding a model id not on this list fails the run immediately with a clear message
# rather than passing it through. That is on purpose: an unlisted id would either
# 400 at the provider or run while tracking as $0 (no pricing row). To add one, put
# it in KNOWN_MODELS (lib/config.ts) AND add a pricing row (lib/llm/pricing.ts) —
# and verify it actually serves the calls first.

# OCR — transcribes image-only / scanned decks into text (only runs when a PDF
# has no selectable text layer).                         [claude-haiku-5-5]     
# OCR_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5

# EXTRACT — reads the deck text and fills in the form fields it states.
#                                                        [claude-haiku-5-5]     
# EXTRACT_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5

# RESEARCH — web-searches to verify/enrich the deck. This is the most expensive
# stage and where model quality matters most.            [claude-haiku-5-5]     
# RESEARCH_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5

# COMPLETE — fills the gaps the deck left, using the research notes.
#                                                        [claude-haiku-5-5]     
# COMPLETE_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5

# SCORECARD — the six 1–5 ratings + funding number.      [claude-sonnet-5-5]    
# SCORECARD_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5

# FEEDBACK — critiques the pitch deck itself.            [claude-sonnet-5-5]    
# FEEDBACK_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5

# LANDSCAPE — researches competitors and maps them on a two-axis grid. Runs in
# parallel with the main pipeline. Claude's web search returns real homepage
# URLs for the competitors' logos and links; Gemini's returns Google redirect
# links.                                 [claude-haiku-5-5]     
# LANDSCAPE_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5


# ─────────────────────────────  Thinking  ───────────────────────────────────
#
# How much each stage's model may think before it answers: minimal, low, medium
# or high. Thinking is billed as output tokens and comes out of the stage's
# output cap, so each step up costs more.
#
# Unset (the default) means the least thinking the stage's model allows. On
# Claude that's effort low with thinking off (Haiku 5.5), thinking only between
# tool calls (Sonnet 5.5), or Opus 5.5's lowest effort, since Opus can't turn
# thinking off; "low" and up turn adaptive thinking on at that effort. On
# Gemini it's "minimal" for every model listed above. A model that can't go as low
# as asked runs at its own minimum instead. Raise one stage at a time, and only
# when its answers need more reasoning than they get by default. Each stage reads
# its own setting:

# OCR_THINKING=minimal
# EXTRACT_THINKING=minimal
# RESEARCH_THINKING=minimal    # on Gemini 2.5, research runs at the model's own default
# COMPLETE_THINKING=minimal
# SCORECARD_THINKING=minimal
# FEEDBACK_THINKING=minimal
# LANDSCAPE_THINKING=minimal   # both landscape calls: the competitor search and the map


# ─────────────────────────  Web-research budget  ────────────────────────────
#
# These control how much web searching the RESEARCH and LANDSCAPE stages do, each
# on its own budget. Lower = cheaper & faster; higher = more thorough. A low web
# presence is reported as a finding, so a small budget doesn't hurt early-stage
# startups.
#
# On Claude (the default) both are hard caps, per search call. Gemini's Google
# Search grounding manages itself and never enforces these, so a stage moved to
# Gemini has the search cap only as a request in the LANDSCAPE prompt, and the
# continuation cap does nothing. Each Claude search is 1¢, the biggest cost of
# an all-Haiku run.

# Max number of web searches per analysis (0–10).                          [3]
# RESEARCH_MAX_SEARCHES=3

# Max times the search loop is allowed to resume to keep digging (0–10).   [3]
# Each resume re-processes the search results so far, so this is a real cost lever.
# RESEARCH_MAX_CONTINUATIONS=3


# ────────────────────────  Advanced: token caps  ────────────────────────────
#
# Max output tokens per stage class. You rarely need to touch these — raise them
# only if you see output getting cut off. (256–128000)

# RESEARCH_MAX_TOKENS=5000     # research notes
# WRITE_MAX_TOKENS=16000       # field-fill + feedback output
# OCR_MAX_TOKENS=16000         # OCR transcription output
